Section 15: Minimal CI Workflow for Unit Tests and Build

Below is a practical GitHub Actions workflow to automatically run unit tests and build the DUME Engine across common environments. It demonstrates a minimal yet effective setup that you can drop into your repository as .github/workflows/ci.yml and adapt as needed.
15.1 Workflow: ci.yml
name: CI - Swift Unit Tests and Build

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main, master ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ main, master ]

jobs:
  swift-linux:
    name: Swift - Linux (Ubuntu)
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Install Swift toolchain
        uses: tráfego/swift-toolchain@v1
        with:
          swift-version: '5.8'  # adjust to your required Swift version

      - name: Cache SwiftPM dependencies
        uses: actions/cache@v4
        with:
          path: |
            ~/.build
            .build
            .package
          key: ${{ runner.os }}-swift-${{ hashFiles('Package.swift', '**/*. Package.resolved') }}

      - name: Resolve dependencies
        run: swift package resolve

      - name: Build
        run: swift build -c release

      - name: Run tests
        run: swift test -c release

  swift-macos:
    name: Swift - macOS
    runs-on: macos-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Resolve dependencies
        run: swift package resolve

      - name: Build
        run: swift build -c release

      - name: Run tests
        run: swift test -c release

15.2 Notes and best practices
    Swift toolchain:
        The Linux job uses a Swift toolchain installer action to ensure a specific Swift version. If your repository relies on a particular Swift version, pin it accordingly (for example, 5.8 or newer). On macOS, the native toolchain is typically sufficient.
    Dependency management:
        This project uses Swift Package Manager (SwiftPM). If you use a different system (CocoaPods, Carthage, etc.), adjust the dependency resolution steps accordingly.
    Caching:
        The Linux job caches build artifacts and dependencies to speed up subsequent runs. If your project structure changes, update the cache paths.
    Testing scope:
        The workflow runs unit tests with -c release to mirror a release-compiled test environment. If your tests rely on debug builds, adjust the configuration accordingly.
    Matrix and coverage:
        For broader coverage, you can extend the workflow with a matrix strategy to test multiple Swift versions or platforms. You can also add code coverage collection and uploading if you have a suitable service.
    Secrets and sensitive data:
        Keep any credentials out of the workflow. If you need API keys or tokens for tests, use GitHub Secrets and reference them in the workflow.

15.3 Quick extension ideas
    Add a linting step with swiftlint (if your project uses SwiftLint).
    Add a documentation build step to ensure docs compile or stay in sync.
    Add a separate job for performance benchmarks to track regressions over time.
